{# Signing in, in an app, with a provider whose page cannot be shown there.

   Apple's and Google's sign-in buttons (login.jinja, account.jinja) are
   links to /auth/apple and /auth/google, which in a browser go to the
   provider's page and come back. In the apps neither can: Google refuses
   its own pages inside an embedded web view (`disallowed_useragent`), and
   either page opened in a browser of the app's answers there, into cookies
   that are not the web view's. So in an app the page takes the press itself
   and goes one of two ways round, chosen here by which app it is in -- the
   app never recognises the link, or knows these paths at all:

   A sheet of the platform's own, which answers with an ID token for a
   nonce: Sign in with Apple on iOS and macOS, Credential Manager for Google
   on Android.
     1. the page asks the site for this sign-in's state and nonce (POST
        /auth/<provider>/start), which the site keeps in the web view's
        session;
     2. it sends the app  signIn {provider, nonce}  on the bridge
        (app_bridge.jinja), and the app answers by calling
        window.oeeeApp.signIn.answer(told), where `told` is {id_token,
        user?} from the sheet, {cancelled: true} for a sheet put away, or {}
        for one that could not sign in;
     3. the page posts the token and the state to /auth/<provider> with
        format=json, and the site checks both against the session
        (src/apple.rs, src/google.rs, identity.rs) and says where to go.

   The system's browser, where there is no sheet: Apple on Android, Google
   on iOS and macOS (in ASWebAuthenticationSession, which Google allows,
   sent straight to Google's page so the sheet names Google), both on
   Windows (Google there straight to Google's page too, which saves the
   browser a stop at this site on its way), and Discord in every app, by
   /auth/discord -- or, on iOS and macOS, straight to Discord's page, so
   the sheet names Discord -- whose sign-in wants the person's Discord
   password or passkey, which the browser has and the web view does not.
   Discord has no sheet anywhere. Steam goes the same way, by /auth/steam, in every
   app but the Steam build (below). Google on iOS and macOS once
   had a sheet of the app's own, with an OAuth client and a token exchange
   of its own to keep; this is the site's own sign-in, in the same system
   sheet.
     1. the page starts a handoff (POST /auth/handoff/start, src/handoff.rs)
        and sends the app  browse {url}  on the bridge; the app checks the
        URL is this site's -- or Google's sign-in page, where the Apple and
        Windows apps are sent straight to, or Discord's, where the Apple
        apps are -- and opens it in the browser, or
        calls window.oeeeApp.signIn.unopened();
     2. the browser signs in, asks the person there whether it was they who
        pressed sign in in the app (identity_handoff_confirm.jinja), and on
        a yes opens oeee-cafe://handoff/done?code=<code>, where <code> is 64
        lowercase hex characters (identity_handoff_done.jinja). The app
        passes it to the page as window.oeeeApp.signIn.resume(code);
     3. the page asks the site (POST /auth/handoff/claim with id, secret
        and the code) until the browser has finished, at which point the
        site signs *this* session in. Without the code the site only ever
        answers "waiting": it is what says the browser that signed in is
        the one beside this app, and not one somebody else was sent a link
        to (src/handoff.rs). The app calls resume(code) when it returned by
        oeee-cafe://handoff/done, and resume() with nothing when it is
        brought back to the front any other way, so the page asks at once
        either way. An app whose sheet closes by itself (iOS and macOS)
        calls unopened() when it was put away -- which the page answers by
        asking once more before letting the handoff go, since the reader
        may have put it away just after signing in.

   Steam, in the build that sells through Steam (data-store="steam"): the
   page sends  signIn {provider: "steam"}, with no nonce, and the app answers
   with window.oeeeApp.signIn.answer({ticket}) -- a Web API ticket, which the
   page posts to /auth/steam -- or {} when Steam would not give one, which
   the page says so about itself.

   Every request is made from here, in the page, so each carries the page's
   cookie and origin. The app never holds the session, sees the handoff's
   secret, or decides where the page goes after. #}
{# The script is frontend/site/signIn.ts, in chrome.js (theme_head.jinja). #}
<script>if (window.oeeeChrome) window.oeeeChrome.signIn({{ ftl_get_message("app-steam-sign-in-failed")|tojson }}, {{ ftl_get_message("ok")|tojson }});</script>
